            "(60-x)*(x-14)=240 simplifies to x^2-74*x+1080=(x-20)*(x-54)=0.",
            "Candidates are reductions (20,2) and (54,-32). The latter enlarges the width to 40 m and violates the required reduction.",
            "The feasible new rectangle is 40 m by 6 m: area 240 m^2 and perimeter 92 m."
          ],
          "algebraic_solution_set": [["20", "2"], ["54", "-32"]],
          "feasible_solution_set": [["20", "2"]],
          "rejected_candidates": [{"pair": ["54", "-32"], "reason": "y=-32 is a width increase, not a reduction; the required 0<y<8 fails."}],
          "result": {"length_reduction_m": "20", "width_reduction_m": "2", "new_length_m": "40", "new_width_m": "6", "new_area_m2": "240", "new_perimeter_m": "92"},
          "completeness_argument": "Both algebraic roots are checked against the original side-specific reduction bounds; exactly one is feasible.",
          "limitations": []
        },
        {
          "part": "б",
          "original_conditions": [
            "Rectangle perimeter 80 m. Increase one side by 8 m and the other by 2 m; area increases by half of the original area.",
            "x+y=40",
            "(x+8)*(y+2)=(3/2)*x*y"
          ],
          "domain": "x>0,y>0 in metres. Define x as the side receiving +8 m and y as the side receiving +2 m; the source does not initially assign these increments to length versus width.",
          "roles": {"x": "original side that receives +8 m", "y": "original side that receives +2 m"},
          "method": [
            "'Increase by half' means final area is 3/2 of the original, not half the original.",
            "Expand to x*y=4*x+16*y+32. With y=40-x, obtain x^2-52*x+672=(x-24)*(x-28)=0.",
            "The increment-labelled pairs are (24,16) and (28,12), both positive.",
            "Both solutions have x>y, so the longer side receives +8 m and the shorter +2 m. If variables instead denote the +2 and +8 recipients, the same rectangles appear as (16,24) and (12,28), not additional shapes."
          ],
          "algebraic_solution_set": [["24", "16"], ["28", "12"]],
          "feasible_solution_set": [["24", "16"], ["28", "12"]],
          "result": {
            "length_width_pairs_m": [["24", "16"], ["28", "12"]],
            "checks": [
              {"original_sides_m": ["24", "16"], "new_sides_m": ["32", "18"], "old_area_m2": "384", "new_area_m2": "576", "increase_m2": "192"},
              {"original_sides_m": ["28", "12"], "new_sides_m": ["36", "14"], "old_area_m2": "336", "new_area_m2": "504", "increase_m2": "168"}
            ]
          },
          "completeness_argument": "The equivalent quadratic has exactly two roots, both physically valid; each shape has perimeter 80 and the required 50% area increase. Swapping the recipient names adds no new shape.",
          "limitations": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "110",
      "group": null,
      "source_pages": ["p0053#2"],
      "status": "ORIGINAL_MATH_SUPPORT_COMPLETE",
      "source_method_expectation": "Use the decimal place-value model and integer digit bounds; do not treat arbitrary real algebraic roots as two-digit numbers.",
      "parts": [
        {
          "part": "а",
          "original_conditions": ["The two-digit number plus twice its digit sum equals 96; the number times its digit sum equals 952.", "N=10*x+y", "S=x+y", "N+2*S=96", "N*S=952"],
          "domain": "x integer in {1,...,9}; y integer in {0,...,9}; N is a positive two-digit integer and 1<=S<=18.",
          "roles": {"x": "tens digit", "y": "units digit", "N": "number", "S": "sum of the digits"},
          "method": [
            "N=96-2*S; substitute into N*S=952 to obtain S^2-48*S+476=(S-14)*(S-34)=0.",
            "S=14 gives N=68, x=(N-S)/9=6 and y=S-x=8.",
            "S=34 gives N=28 and the algebraic digits (-2/3,104/3); S>18 and both digit bounds fail.",
            "Verify 68+2*(6+8)=96 and 68*(6+8)=952."
          ],
          "algebraic_solution_set": [["-2/3", "104/3"], ["6", "8"]],
          "feasible_solution_set": [["6", "8"]],
          "rejected_candidates": [{"pair": ["-2/3", "104/3"], "reason": "Not integer decimal digits; tens digit is negative, units digit exceeds 9, and digit sum 34 exceeds 18."}],
          "result": {"two_digit_number": 68, "tens_digit": 6, "units_digit": 8, "digit_sum": 14},
          "completeness_argument": "Both roots for S are considered and only one survives the exact original digit domain; direct digit enumeration agrees.",
          "limitations": []
        },
        {
          "part": "б",
          "original_conditions": [
            "The sum of the squares of the digits of a two-digit number is 65. After adding 27, the resulting number has the same digits as the original.",
            "x^2+y^2=65",
            "10*x+y+27=10*y+x"
          ],
          "domain": "x integer in {1,...,9}; y integer in {0,...,9}; for the changed number to have the same two digits in reverse order its tens digit y must also be nonzero.",
          "roles": {"x": "original tens digit", "y": "original units digit"},
          "interpretation": "Same digits means the same multiset of the two digits. Since adding 27 gives a different number, retaining the order is impossible; for two digits the only other order is reversal. This is not merely an unchanged digit sum.",
          "method": [
            "The reversal equation gives 9*(y-x)=27, so y=x+3.",
            "x^2+(x+3)^2=65 gives x^2+3*x-28=(x-4)*(x+7)=0.",
            "The real candidates are digits (4,7) and (-7,-4); only (4,7) lies in the digit domain.",
            "47+27=74 uses exactly the same two digits; 4^2+7^2=65."
          ],
          "algebraic_solution_set": [["-7", "-4"], ["4", "7"]],
          "feasible_solution_set": [["4", "7"]],
          "rejected_candidates": [{"pair": ["-7", "-4"], "reason": "Negative values are not decimal digits."}],
          "result": {"two_digit_number": 47, "number_after_adding_27": 74, "tens_digit": 4, "units_digit": 7},
          "completeness_argument": "The changed two-digit number can only be the reversal; both quadratic roots are considered, and finite digit enumeration yields only (4,7).",
          "limitations": ["The ordinary same-digit-multiset interpretation is stated explicitly; no raw-source review was performed to replace or rewrite the wording."]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "111",
      "group": null,
      "source_pages": ["p0053#3"],
      "status": "ORIGINAL_MATH_SUPPORT_COMPLETE",
      "source_method_expectation": "Use the sum of speeds for the meeting and distance/speed for full-trip times; keep which traveller arrives earlier or later.",
      "parts": [
        {
          "part": "а",
          "original_conditions": [
            "Cyclists depart simultaneously from A and B, 28 km apart, toward each other and meet after 1 hour.",
            "The first cyclist from A reaches B 35 minutes later than the second from B reaches A.",
            "x+y=28",
            "28/x-28/y=35/60=7/12"
          ],
          "domain": "x>0,y>0 in km/h; x<y because the first cyclist takes longer over the same 28 km. Constant speeds and no stopping are implicit.",
          "roles": {"x": "speed of first cyclist, A to B (km/h)", "y": "speed of second cyclist, B to A (km/h)"},
          "method": [
            "Clearing positive denominators gives 48*(y-x)=x*y.",
            "With y=28-x, x^2-124*x+1344=(x-12)*(x-112)=0.",
            "Candidates (12,16) and (112,-84); reject the negative second speed.",
            "At (12,16), meeting distances are 12 and 16 km. Full-trip times are 28/12=7/3 h=140 min and 28/16=7/4 h=105 min."
          ],
          "algebraic_solution_set": [["12", "16"], ["112", "-84"]],
          "feasible_solution_set": [["12", "16"]],
          "rejected_candidates": [{"pair": ["112", "-84"], "reason": "Second speed is negative and is incompatible with travelling toward the other town."}],
          "result": {"first_from_A_kmh": "12", "second_from_B_kmh": "16", "first_full_trip_minutes": "140", "second_full_trip_minutes": "105", "first_arrives_later_by_minutes": "35"},
          "completeness_argument": "Both algebraic roots are enumerated; exactly one has two positive speeds and the correct later-arrival role.",
          "limitations": []
        },
        {
          "part": "б",
          "original_conditions": [
            "Trains depart simultaneously from M and N, 45 km apart, toward each other and meet after 20 minutes.",
            "The train from M reaches N 9 minutes earlier than the train from N reaches M.",
            "(x+y)*(20/60)=45",
            "45/y-45/x=9/60=3/20"
          ],
          "domain": "x>0,y>0 in km/h; x>y because the M train arrives earlier over the same distance. Constant speeds are implicit.",
          "roles": {"x": "speed of train from M toward N (km/h)", "y": "speed of train from N toward M (km/h)"},
          "method": [
            "Meeting condition gives x+y=135. The signed full-trip difference gives 300*(x-y)=x*y.",
            "Set y=135-x; x^2+465*x-40500=(x-75)*(x+540)=0.",
            "Candidates (75,60) and (-540,675); reject the negative M-train speed.",
            "At (75,60), 20-minute distances are 25 and 20 km. Full-trip times are 45/75=3/5 h=36 min and 45/60=3/4 h=45 min."
          ],
          "algebraic_solution_set": [["-540", "675"], ["75", "60"]],
          "feasible_solution_set": [["75", "60"]],
          "rejected_candidates": [{"pair": ["-540", "675"], "reason": "M-train speed is negative and does not represent the specified journey."}],
          "result": {"from_M_kmh": "75", "from_N_kmh": "60", "M_train_full_trip_minutes": "36", "N_train_full_trip_minutes": "45", "M_train_arrives_earlier_by_minutes": "9"},
          "completeness_argument": "Both real algebraic roots are considered; exactly one satisfies positivity and the earlier-arrival role of the M train.",
          "limitations": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "112",
      "group": null,
      "source_pages": ["p0053#4"],
      "status": "ORIGINAL_MATH_SUPPORT_COMPLETE",
      "source_method_expectation": "Use reciprocal solo times as work rates; half a tank takes half the corresponding solo filling time.",
      "parts": [
        {
          "part": "а",
          "original_conditions": [
            "The two pipes together fill one tank in 12 minutes. One pipe fills half a tank and then the other fills the other half; total sequential time is 25 minutes.",
            "1/x+1/y=1/12",
            "x/2+y/2=25"
          ],
          "domain": "x>0,y>0 in minutes; x,y are each pipe's solo full-tank time. Constant rates, an initially empty tank, and no leakage are implicit.",
          "roles": {"x": "first pipe solo full-tank time (min)", "y": "second pipe solo full-tank time (min)"},
          "method": [
            "The half-tank condition gives x+y=50. The combined-rate condition gives (x+y)/(x*y)=1/12, hence x*y=600.",
            "u^2-50*u+600=(u-20)*(u-30)=0.",
            "Solo times are 20 and 30 min; half-tank times are 10 and 15 min, adding to 25. The combined rate 1/20+1/30=1/12 gives 12 min."
          ],
          "algebraic_solution_set": [["20", "30"], ["30", "20"]],
          "feasible_solution_set": [["20", "30"], ["30", "20"]],
          "result": {"unordered_solo_times_minutes": ["20", "30"]},
          "completeness_argument": "Both positive label assignments are feasible; the symmetric source conditions do not identify which pipe is faster.",
          "limitations": []
        },
        {
          "part": "б",
          "original_conditions": [
            "One combine completes the same field 24 hours faster than the other. Together they finish in 35 hours.",
            "y-x=24",
            "1/x+1/y=1/35"
          ],
          "domain": "x>0,y>0 in hours, with x<y; the machines work at constant rates on the same full-field job.",
          "roles": {"x": "faster combine solo completion time (h)", "y": "slower combine solo completion time (h)"},
          "method": [
            "Set y=x+24; clearing positive denominators gives 35*(2*x+24)=x*(x+24).",
            "x^2-46*x-840=(x-60)*(x+14)=0.",
            "Candidates (60,84) and (-14,10); reject the negative solo time.",
            "84-60=24 and 1/60+1/84=1/35."
          ],
          "algebraic_solution_set": [["-14", "10"], ["60", "84"]],
          "feasible_solution_set": [["60", "84"]],
          "rejected_candidates": [{"pair": ["-14", "10"], "reason": "Negative completion time has no feasible work-rate meaning."}],
          "result": {"faster_combine_hours": "60", "slower_combine_hours": "84", "together_hours": "35"},
          "completeness_argument": "Both algebraic roots are retained for screening; exactly one supplies two positive times with the specified faster/slower roles.",
          "limitations": []
        }
      ]
    },
